# A WebRTC call needs three things to work: STUN servers that tell each peer
# its public address, TURN servers that relay media when no direct path
# exists, and a signaling channel to trade offers and answers.
#
# STUN and TURN requests take the same URLs as RTCPeerConnection's iceServers.
# The response is a report: `mapped` is the address the server saw, `relayed`
# the relay a TURN server handed out, `rtt` the round trip in milliseconds.
# A TURN server that rejects the credentials answers 401.

### Public address from Google
STUN stun:stun.l.google.com:19302

> assert status == 200
> assert body.mapped.port > 0
> assert body.rtt < 500
> print "reachable as " + body.mapped.address

### Public address from Cloudflare
STUN stun:stun.cloudflare.com:3478

> assert status == 200

### TURN relay
# Without credentials this proves the server is up and asks for them. Put
# turnUser and turnPassword in environment.local.stamp and enable the @auth
# line to allocate a real relay, then assert on body.relayed.
# @auth basic {{turnUser}} {{secret(turnPassword)}}
TURN turns:turn.cloudflare.com:5349

> assert status == 401
> assert body.realm != null
> print body.relayed ?? "relay needs credentials for " + body.realm

### Signaling
# Peers usually swap SDP over a WebSocket. This one bounces the offer straight
# back, which is enough to check the messages survive the trip.
WS wss://ws.postman-echo.com/raw

> let offer = { type: "offer", sdp: "v=0\r\no=- " + randomInt(100000, 999999) + " 2 IN IP4 127.0.0.1\r\ns=-\r\nt=0 0\r\n", from: uuid() }
> send offer
> receive 5s
> assert data.type == "offer"
> assert data.sdp == offer.sdp
> close
